1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,310
In a quiet town in the Netherlands, there
is a machine about the size of a bus.

2
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:10,852
It is assembled from roughly a hundred
thousand parts, sourced from more than

3
00:00:10,852 --> 00:00:13,327
five thousand suppliers across twenty-nine
countries.

4
00:00:13,507 --> 00:00:17,977
It ships in two hundred and fifty crates,
and it takes months to install.

5
00:00:18,157 --> 00:00:21,512
Exactly one company on Earth knows how to
build it.

6
00:00:21,692 --> 00:00:26,949
And without it, the most advanced computer
chips in the world do not get made. Not in

7
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:28,495
America. Not in China. Nowhere.

8
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:33,645
This is the story of the narrowest
bottleneck in the global economy, and why,

9
00:00:33,645 --> 00:00:37,905
after hundreds of billions of dollars,
nobody has managed to widen it.

10
00:00:40,555 --> 00:00:45,584
To understand why that machine matters,
you have to understand what a modern chip

11
00:00:45,584 --> 00:00:46,302
actually is.

12
00:00:46,482 --> 00:00:52,191
Inside the phone in your pocket is a piece
of silicon about the size of a fingernail.

13
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:55,213
Printed onto it are more than fifteen
billion transistors.

14
00:00:55,513 --> 00:01:00,666
A transistor is just a switch. It lets
current through, or it doesn't. That's the

15
00:01:00,666 --> 00:01:01,353
whole trick.

16
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:06,972
Wire enough of them together and you get
arithmetic. Then logic. Then memory. Then

17
00:01:06,972 --> 00:01:07,361
everything.

18
00:01:07,661 --> 00:01:12,466
And the entire history of computing is one
move, repeated: make the switches smaller,

19
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:16,241
so you can fit more of them in the same
space.

20
00:01:16,421 --> 00:01:22,849
In 1971, the features were ten thousand
nanometers across. Today the leading edge

21
00:01:22,849 --> 00:01:24,826
is called three nanometer.

22
00:01:25,006 --> 00:01:29,858
That name is now mostly marketing, but the
real structures are only a few dozen atoms

23
00:01:29,858 --> 00:01:30,161
wide.

24
00:01:30,511 --> 00:01:35,341
At that scale you are not really
manufacturing any more. You are doing

25
00:01:35,341 --> 00:01:39,799
applied physics, trillions of times a
month, with almost no mistakes allowed.

26
00:01:39,979 --> 00:01:41,488
And the way you do it is with light.

27
00:01:41,888 --> 00:01:44,640
Chips are printed, a little like
photographs.

28
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:50,712
You take a blueprint of the circuit,
called a mask, shine light through it, and

29
00:01:50,712 --> 00:01:54,247
project the pattern onto silicon coated in
light-sensitive chemicals.

30
00:01:54,427 --> 00:01:59,818
Wash away what the light touched. Etch
what's left. Then do it again. Eighty, a

31
00:01:59,818 --> 00:02:01,974
hundred times over, layer on layer.

32
00:02:02,274 --> 00:02:05,679
The catch is that you cannot print a
feature much smaller than the wavelength

33
00:02:05,679 --> 00:02:07,138
of the light you print with.

34
00:02:07,318 --> 00:02:12,642
For decades the industry used deep
ultraviolet light, at a hundred and

35
00:02:12,642 --> 00:02:13,530
ninety-three nanometers.

36
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:18,287
And for a long time, clever tricks kept
that going. Shooting the light through

37
00:02:18,287 --> 00:02:19,879
water to bend it tighter.

38
00:02:20,059 --> 00:02:25,546
Exposing the same wafer two, three, four
times with slightly offset masks, to draw

39
00:02:25,546 --> 00:02:28,290
lines finer than the light should allow.

40
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:34,088
But every trick costs money, time, and
yield. By the twenty-tens, the industry

41
00:02:34,088 --> 00:02:36,249
needed shorter light. Much shorter.

42
00:02:36,549 --> 00:02:41,077
The answer was thirteen point five
nanometers. Extreme ultraviolet.

43
00:02:41,257 --> 00:02:46,231
And here is the problem with extreme
ultraviolet: almost nothing reflects it,

44
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:48,304
and almost everything absorbs it.

45
00:02:48,484 --> 00:02:54,088
Glass absorbs it. Air absorbs it. You
cannot use a lens. You cannot even use an

46
00:02:54,088 --> 00:02:54,788
ordinary mirror.

47
00:02:54,968 --> 00:02:57,534
So this is what had to be built instead.

48
00:02:57,834 --> 00:03:02,823
A stream of molten tin droplets falls
through a vacuum chamber. About fifty

49
00:03:02,823 --> 00:03:04,742
thousand of them, every second.

50
00:03:04,922 --> 00:03:09,856
Each droplet is hit by a high-power carbon
dioxide laser. Twice.

51
00:03:10,036 --> 00:03:15,163
The first pulse flattens it into a
pancake. The second vaporises it into a

52
00:03:15,163 --> 00:03:18,825
plasma hot enough to glow at thirteen
point five nanometers.

53
00:03:19,125 --> 00:03:23,838
That light is gathered not by lenses but
by mirrors, and not ordinary mirrors

54
00:03:23,838 --> 00:03:24,175
either.

55
00:03:24,355 --> 00:03:29,414
They are built from dozens of alternating
layers of molybdenum and silicon, each

56
00:03:29,414 --> 00:03:35,251
only a few atoms thick, tuned so the weak
reflections add up instead of cancelling

57
00:03:35,251 --> 00:03:35,640
out.

58
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:40,701
Even so, each mirror returns only about
seventy percent of the light that hits it.

59
00:03:40,701 --> 00:03:46,883
Put ten in a row and most of your beam is
gone, so the source has to be brutally

60
00:03:46,883 --> 00:03:47,860
overpowered to compensate.

61
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,516
The mirrors are ground in Germany to a
precision that is genuinely hard to

62
00:03:52,516 --> 00:03:52,827
picture.

63
00:03:53,007 --> 00:03:57,521
The manufacturer's own comparison: scale
one of these mirrors up to the size of

64
00:03:57,521 --> 00:04:02,358
Germany, and the largest bump on its
surface would be about a tenth of a

65
00:04:02,358 --> 00:04:03,003
millimetre tall.

66
00:04:03,353 --> 00:04:08,232
All of it sits in a vacuum, on a stage
that moves the wafer at speed while

67
00:04:08,232 --> 00:04:10,528
holding position to a fraction of a
nanometer.

68
00:04:10,708 --> 00:04:15,642
And it is expected to run, profitably,
around the clock, for years.

69
00:04:15,822 --> 00:04:20,559
The newest generation reportedly sells for
over three hundred and fifty million

70
00:04:20,559 --> 00:04:25,691
euros. Fabs buy them anyway, because the
alternative is not competing at all.

71
00:04:25,991 --> 00:04:29,683
This is why there is only one supplier. It
isn't a patent.

72
00:04:29,863 --> 00:04:34,886
It is thirty years of accumulated, mostly
undocumented practical knowledge, spread

73
00:04:34,886 --> 00:04:40,822
across thousands of specialised firms,
each of which is itself hard to replace.

74
00:04:41,002 --> 00:04:44,741
You cannot buy that. You cannot really
steal it. You have to grow it.

75
00:04:45,141 --> 00:04:48,276
But having the machine is not the same as
being able to use it.

76
00:04:48,456 --> 00:04:52,322
Which brings us to the second chokepoint,
and this one is on an island.

77
00:04:52,502 --> 00:04:57,149
Until the nineteen-eighties, if you
designed chips, you also built them.

78
00:04:57,149 --> 00:05:00,106
Designers and factories lived under one
roof.

79
00:05:00,286 --> 00:05:06,364
Then in nineteen eighty-seven, a Taiwanese
company made a strange bet. It would build

80
00:05:06,364 --> 00:05:11,574
chips and only build chips, never
designing anything that competed with its

81
00:05:11,574 --> 00:05:12,442
own customers.

82
00:05:12,742 --> 00:05:18,170
That company was TSMC, and the bet
reorganised the whole industry. Designers

83
00:05:18,170 --> 00:05:24,503
like Apple, Nvidia, Qualcomm and AMD could
now exist without owning a single factory.

84
00:05:24,683 --> 00:05:31,625
The result: the hardest part of the supply
chain collapsed into very few hands. By

85
00:05:31,625 --> 00:05:36,717
twenty twenty-five, TSMC held roughly
seventy percent of the entire contract

86
00:05:36,717 --> 00:05:38,568
chipmaking market by revenue.

87
00:05:38,748 --> 00:05:43,656
At the leading edge the concentration is
sharper still. Samsung has fought with

88
00:05:43,656 --> 00:05:48,941
yields. Intel spent years behind. For the
most advanced chips on Earth, there is

89
00:05:48,941 --> 00:05:50,451
effectively one real option.

90
00:05:50,751 --> 00:05:55,151
And the reason is boring, which is exactly
what makes it so hard to beat. Yield.

91
00:05:55,331 --> 00:06:01,839
Every fab loses some chips to defects. If
your rival gets eighty good chips from a

92
00:06:01,839 --> 00:06:07,127
wafer and you get fifty, you lose. Same
machines, same design, same everything.

93
00:06:07,307 --> 00:06:12,098
Yield comes from running millions of
wafers and fixing thousands of tiny

94
00:06:12,098 --> 00:06:18,086
problems you only find by running millions
of wafers. It is a flywheel: volume buys

95
00:06:18,086 --> 00:06:19,683
learning, learning buys volume.

96
00:06:19,863 --> 00:06:25,601
TSMC has been spinning that flywheel for
nearly forty years, mostly within a few

97
00:06:25,601 --> 00:06:28,060
dozen miles of Taiwan's west coast.

98
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:34,556
In twenty twenty, the world ran a small,
accidental experiment in what happens when

99
00:06:34,556 --> 00:06:35,883
this system hiccups.

100
00:06:36,063 --> 00:06:40,643
When the pandemic hit, carmakers cancelled
their chip orders. Consumer electronics

101
00:06:40,643 --> 00:06:45,639
demand exploded and took the freed-up
capacity. When the carmakers came back,

102
00:06:45,639 --> 00:06:48,138
the queue had closed behind them.

103
00:06:48,318 --> 00:06:54,169
These were not advanced chips. Many were
cheap, old, unglamorous parts. It made no

104
00:06:54,169 --> 00:06:54,587
difference.

105
00:06:54,767 --> 00:06:59,112
By the end of twenty twenty-one, the
consultancy AlixPartners estimated the

106
00:06:59,112 --> 00:07:04,643
shortage would cost the car industry two
hundred and ten billion dollars in lost

107
00:07:04,643 --> 00:07:09,384
revenue, and about seven point seven
million vehicles that were simply never

108
00:07:09,384 --> 00:07:09,779
built.

109
00:07:09,959 --> 00:07:14,849
Lead times stretched past twenty weeks.
Assembly lines sat idle, waiting on

110
00:07:14,849 --> 00:07:18,109
components worth less than a cup of
coffee.

111
00:07:18,289 --> 00:07:23,525
That was the system working, more or less.
Just with the timing slightly off.

112
00:07:23,825 --> 00:07:26,042
So: what if the island stopped?

113
00:07:26,222 --> 00:07:30,328
The estimates here carry enormous
uncertainty, and anyone who tells you

114
00:07:30,328 --> 00:07:33,688
otherwise is selling something. But they
are not small.

115
00:07:33,868 --> 00:07:39,632
Rhodium Group put over two trillion
dollars of annual economic activity at

116
00:07:39,632 --> 00:07:44,436
risk in a blockade scenario, before
counting sanctions, escalation, or

117
00:07:44,436 --> 00:07:45,396
knock-on effects.

118
00:07:45,576 --> 00:07:51,188
Bloomberg Economics modelled a year-long
blockade and got roughly five percent off

119
00:07:51,188 --> 00:07:56,333
global output, around five trillion
dollars, with Taiwan down twelve percent,

120
00:07:56,333 --> 00:08:00,542
China down nearly nine, the United States
down three.

121
00:08:00,722 --> 00:08:04,460
For a full war, their figure was closer to
ten trillion.

122
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:09,683
For scale: the two thousand eight
financial crisis took a little over three

123
00:08:09,683 --> 00:08:11,235
percent off global output.

124
00:08:11,584 --> 00:08:15,137
And unlike oil, you cannot stockpile your
way out of this.

125
00:08:15,317 --> 00:08:20,340
A chip design is tied to one factory's
specific process. You cannot take the

126
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:22,492
blueprint somewhere else and press print.

127
00:08:22,892 --> 00:08:27,809
So for the past few years, governments
have been trying to fix this. Two

128
00:08:27,809 --> 00:08:29,916
strategies, running at the same time.

129
00:08:30,096 --> 00:08:33,092
Strategy one: build it somewhere else.

130
00:08:33,272 --> 00:08:38,875
In twenty twenty-two the United States
passed the CHIPS and Science Act. Around

131
00:08:38,875 --> 00:08:43,185
fifty-three billion dollars, of which
roughly thirty-nine billion was direct

132
00:08:43,185 --> 00:08:47,064
manufacturing incentives, plus tens of
billions in lending authority.

133
00:08:47,244 --> 00:08:52,721
Europe, Japan, South Korea, India and
China have all announced versions of the

134
00:08:52,721 --> 00:08:59,041
same idea. TSMC is building in Arizona and
Japan. Intel and Samsung are expanding in

135
00:08:59,041 --> 00:09:00,305
the United States.

136
00:09:00,605 --> 00:09:03,392
Strategy two: stop the competition.

137
00:09:03,572 --> 00:09:07,764
In October twenty twenty-two, the United
States introduced sweeping export controls

138
00:09:07,764 --> 00:09:13,861
designed to cut China off from advanced
chips and the tools that make them. A year

139
00:09:13,861 --> 00:09:16,529
later the rules were tightened and
widened.

140
00:09:16,709 --> 00:09:21,120
An EUV machine has never been allowed to
be sold to China. Not one.

141
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:22,755
So how is it going?

142
00:09:22,935 --> 00:09:28,150
Partly. Fabs genuinely are being built,
and some are producing. But the fab is the

143
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:28,845
easy part.

144
00:09:29,025 --> 00:09:34,674
What's hard to move is everything around
it. The specialty chemical suppliers. The

145
00:09:34,674 --> 00:09:38,150
equipment service engineers. The packaging
and testing plants.

146
00:09:38,330 --> 00:09:43,252
And the particular culture of a workforce
that will shut down and restart a

147
00:09:43,252 --> 00:09:47,119
two-billion-dollar line at three in the
morning because one sensor drifted.

148
00:09:47,299 --> 00:09:52,627
And underneath every one of these plans,
in Arizona and Dresden and Kumamoto alike,

149
00:09:52,627 --> 00:09:57,574
sits the same machine, from the same
company, in the same Dutch town.

150
00:09:57,754 --> 00:10:02,789
Diversifying the factories does not
diversify the chokepoint. It just gives it

151
00:10:02,789 --> 00:10:03,629
more customers.

152
00:10:03,929 --> 00:10:08,027
Meanwhile the controls have had an effect,
but not a clean one.

153
00:10:08,207 --> 00:10:13,683
In twenty twenty-three, Huawei shipped a
phone with a seven-nanometer-class chip

154
00:10:13,683 --> 00:10:20,155
built by China's SMIC, without EUV, using
older machines and many more exposure

155
00:10:20,155 --> 00:10:20,653
passes.

156
00:10:20,833 --> 00:10:25,957
Analysts believe the yields are poor and
the costs high. But it exists. Impossible

157
00:10:25,957 --> 00:10:27,787
turned out to mean expensive.

158
00:10:27,967 --> 00:10:32,153
Which is the uncomfortable thing about
chokepoints. Squeezing one does not only

159
00:10:32,153 --> 00:10:35,641
stop your rival. It tells them exactly
where to dig.

160
00:10:36,041 --> 00:10:40,937
None of this happened because of a
conspiracy, or a failure. It happened

161
00:10:40,937 --> 00:10:42,067
because of specialisation.

162
00:10:42,247 --> 00:10:47,021
Every step — splitting design from
manufacturing, buying the one best

163
00:10:47,021 --> 00:10:52,229
machine, clustering the factories where
the expertise already was — made chips

164
00:10:52,229 --> 00:10:57,003
cheaper and better, faster than almost
anything humans have ever built.

165
00:10:57,183 --> 00:11:02,118
The fragility was not the price of getting
it wrong. It was the price of getting it

166
00:11:02,118 --> 00:11:02,698
relentlessly right.

167
00:11:02,878 --> 00:11:07,819
We built the most sophisticated supply
chain in history, and we optimised it

168
00:11:07,819 --> 00:11:11,620
until there was almost no slack left
anywhere in it.

169
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:17,440
And now the whole digital world balances
on a few thousand people in Taiwan, a few

170
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:22,728
hundred in a Dutch town, and a stream of
tin droplets falling through a vacuum,

171
00:11:22,728 --> 00:11:24,490
fifty thousand times a second.
